const abnormCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · What "abnormal" means — carefully', note: 'Distress, dysfunction, danger — the 3 Ds. Deviance alone isn\'t disorder (genius is deviant); the classification is about impairment and suffering, and its history includes removed diagnoses (homosexuality, 1973).' },
    { math: '2 · The diagnostic system (DSM/ICD)', note: 'Categories from descriptive criteria, not causes. Reliability is good; validity is debated — most disorders share symptoms and risk genes ("heterogeneity and comorbidity"), pushing research toward dimensions.' },
    { math: '3 · The therapies, by mechanism', note: 'CBT (change thought-behaviour loops — best evidence for anxiety, depression), exposure (phobias, PTSD), medication (neurochemical), interpersonal therapy, mindfulness-based approaches. Different disorders respond to different tools.' },
    { math: '4 · What works: the honest meta-analysis', note: 'Psychotherapy works (average effect ~0.8 SD) and most bona fide therapies work similarly for many conditions — the "dodo bird verdict". Specific factors matter for specific disorders; the therapeutic alliance predicts outcome across all of them.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/abnormal-psychology-therapies">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Abnormal psychology studies <strong>mental disorder: its definitions, causes and
    treatments</strong> — a field where the object of study is suffering itself. The modern
    position is diagnostic humility (categories are descriptions, not essences) and treatment
    pluralism (evidence-based therapy, medication, or both — matched to condition and person).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="definition, diagnosis, therapy mechanisms, and the evidence verdict" widget="equationStep" config={abnormCfg} />

  <h2>The major conditions, briefly and precisely</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Depression:</strong> ~280 million people worldwide; treated by CBT, interpersonal therapy, SSRIs, exercise (real effect); behavioural activation (re-engaging with life) is the deceptively simple core.</li>
    <li><strong>Anxiety disorders:</strong> the most common category; exposure therapy is the star — systematically facing the feared stimulus until extinction learning wins. Avoidance maintains; exposure dismantles.</li>
    <li><strong>Schizophrenia:</strong> ~1% worldwide across cultures; dopamine dysregulation + neurodevelopmental factors; antipsychotics treat positive symptoms (hallucinations), while social support and cognitive remediation address the rest. Outcome inequality is a treatment-access story.</li>
    <li><strong>ADHD and autism:</strong> neurodevelopmental conditions, heavily reframed in recent decades — the neurodiversity movement's contribution is real (accommodation research), while clinical needs remain clinical.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> DSM-5-TR; Barlow, Handbook of Clinical Psychology; Wampold & Imel, The Great Psychotherapy Debate; Cuijpers meta-analyses.</p>
</TopicPage>
